  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 151 PM MDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\nGlobal ensemble forecast guidance remains generally unchanged \ncompared to yesterday regarding the long-term forecast period from \nthis weekend into next week. High confidence exists for tranquil \nweather through much of the long-term period. Large-scale ridging \nwill dominate the weather pattern over the western CONUS resulting \nin generally mild above-average temperatures and limited \nprecipitation chances over southeast Wyoming and western Nebraska. \nThis ridging pattern continues to be a result of the highly active \ntropical pattern over the eastern Pacific. Numerous EPAC and CPAC \ntropical storms will continue to move west and then recurve to the \nnorth in the mid-latitudes of the central Pacific, resulting in \npotent synoptic troughing over the Bearing Sea/Gulf of Alaska. This \nsetup then drives downstream ridging over western North America, \nwhich is apparent once again in today's ensemble mean guidance. As \nmentioned yesterday, these types of stable-synoptic-wave patterns \ncan often persist for weeks or more, locking in high-confidence \nforecasts over much of the west including our region. \n\nLocal details remain largely unchanged in the forecast. Above-\naverage temps are expected each day from Saturday onward. High \nconfidence exists in temps around 5 degrees above average this \nweekend (low to mid-70s east of the mountains) and highs 10 to 15 \ndegrees above average through next week. The one new exception in \nthe forecast in today's guidance is a very weak wave in the overall \nridge which may move through on Tuesday. Ensemble guidance has a \nvery high amount of spatiotemporal dispersion with regard to this \nsystem. Some members do not even show this wave, and others bring it \nthrough strong enough to produce some weak showers over the higher \nterrain of southern Wyoming and northern Colorado. We will opt for \nthe driest solutions for now given recent model performance with \nsimilar systems. Mild temps even during the overnight hours should \ncontinue to hold off the first frosts for any populated areas \noutside of the mountains. Afternoon RH values will drop to near or \nslightly below 20% throughout the weekend during the afternoon hours \nacross all of southeast Wyoming and portions of the Nebraska \npanhandle. However, generally light surface flow out of the west \nless than 20 mph will likely preclude the need for any fire weather \nheadlines. The synoptic pattern described in both today's and \nyesterdays discussion will continue to result in high forecast \nconfidence throughout the entire long-term period with limited \nsocietal impacts expected.",
